The Jevons Paradox is a counterintuitive economic theory: as technological advancements make a resource more efficient to use, the total consumption of that resource increases rather than decreases. [1, 2, 3] 
Instead of conserving the resource, the efficiency gains act like a massive price cut, unlocking explosive new demand. [1, 4] 
------------------------------
## 📜 The Historical Origin
In his 1865 book The Coal Question, British economist William Stanley Jevons noticed a strange trend. James Watt had introduced a vastly more fuel-efficient steam engine. You might expect that because engines needed less coal to do the same amount of work, England’s total coal consumption would plummet. [2, 4, 5] 
Instead, the exact opposite happened: coal consumption surged. [5] 
Because running a steam engine became dramatically cheaper and more practical, thousands of factories adopted them. Entirely new industries emerged, and the collective demand for coal skyrocketed, vastly outstripping the savings from the efficiency upgrade. Economists call this result the rebound effect. [2, 5, 6] 
------------------------------
## 🤖 How It Applies to Modern AI
Technology leaders and economists frequently invoke the Jevons Paradox because the exact same pattern is playing out with artificial intelligence. [4, 5, 7] 

| Traditional View of AI | The Jevons Paradox Reality |
|---|---|
| Assumption: AI makes cognitive work faster, so companies will need fewer workers and less compute to do the same amount of work. | Outcome: Collapsing costs spark completely new use cases. Total aggregate demand for both computing power and workers expands exponentially. |

This paradox manifests across the AI landscape in three major ways:
## 1. The Compute and Energy Explosion
As AI chips become more efficient and engineers optimize algorithms (such as the shift toward fast "System One" decision models like Jev), the cost per unit of compute drops drastically. However, rather than scaling back data centers to save money, companies simply run models more frequently, across deeper pipelines, and for entirely new applications. Efficiency gains actually drive higher net demands on hardware and energy grids. [8, 9, 10, 11, 12] 
## 2. The Software Engineering Paradox
AI coding assistants make a single developer far more efficient. If the global demand for software were fixed, this would lead to massive layoffs. However, because writing software has become dramatically cheaper, companies are tackling endless backlogs, building hyper-personalized user experiences, and embedding code into physical products where it was previously cost-prohibitive. The result? The software industry is expanding, keeping demand for software engineers high. [13] 
## 3. Shift in Knowledge Work (The Cognition Market)
When the cost of accessing expert-level information falls, organizations do not stop generating research—they generate more of it. They build more prototypes, run more strategic simulations, and increase their iteration speed. As automated execution becomes a cheap commodity, the market value shifts heavily toward human workers who possess the judgment, critical thinking, and domain expertise required to direct and audit these AI-driven outputs. [5, 14, 15, 16] 
